  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nSlightly cooler weather and low rain chances (10-30%) for areas \nsouth of Interstate 10 kick off the long-term period. This is due to \nan inverted upper-level trough developing over the forecast area on \nThursday. A tropical disturbance in the southwest Gulf along with \nsoutheasterly to easterly winds bring surface moisture to the far \nsouthernmost portions of the region. As a result, low chances for \nisolated to scattered showers/storms are in store Thursday and \nFriday across the Big Bend, Lower Trans Pecos, and Davis Mountains. \nPWATs are forecast to hover near the 75th climatological percentile \nindicating the potential for heavy rainfall. Given the limited \nshower/storm coverage, the flash flood threat remains very low. \nSurface high pressure from the Rockies will keep dry air in the \nlower levels for areas north of the aforementioned areas, resulting \nin slim to none rain/storm chances (< 10%). Slightly cooler \ntemperatures are expected through Saturday ranging from the mid 70s \nto mid 80s for most locations, thanks to the aforementioned inverted \ntrough. By Saturday, the trough lifts northeast of the region and \nsubtle upper-level ridging builds back in from the west. This sets \nup a warming trend late in the weekend into early next week with \ntemperatures rebounding back into the mid 80s to low 90s regionwide. \n\nLamberson",
